\section{URL enumeration}{

The list endpoint is
\verb{GET /umbraco/api/license/getall?type=0&caseType=00&county=00&filterText=&municipality=00&pageNumber=N&pageSize=M}
(JSON). Its \code{Licenses} array carries the case records; \code{Counties},
\code{Municipalities}, \code{CaseTypes}, and \code{LicenseStatuses} are filter-vocab facets
returned inline, and \code{TotalCount} is the count for the requested filter.
\strong{\code{type=0} ("Alle typer") is load-bearing}: the API's server-side default for
a \emph{missing} \code{type} is \code{V-1} (Vannkraft / hydro), so omitting it would crawl
only the ~7 300 hydro cases. Sending \code{type=0} returns all ~11 000+ cases
across every technology. The other technology codes are \code{V-1} Vannkraft,
\code{A-6} Vindkraft, \code{A-8} Solkraft, \code{A-9} Havvind, \code{A-1} Nett, \code{A-7} Fjernvarme,
\code{A-5} Andre energianlegg, plus \code{V-9}/\code{V-19}; a record's own code(s) are kept
in \code{case_type_code} (occasionally comma-joined, e.g. \code{"A-1,A-8"}) for
downstream narrowing. The other defaults \verb{caseType=00&county=00&municipality=00&filterText=}
mean "all". \code{pageSize} (default 200, overridable via
\code{getOption("planscanR.no_page_size")}) batches the listing so the crawl makes
a few dozen list GETs rather than ~1 150 at the 10-row server default — a real
saving under the 20 s crawl-delay. The page generator (\code{no_fetch_search()})
paginates \verb{pageNumber = 1, 2, …} until a page returns no \code{Licenses} (parsed
with \code{perform_json}). The canonical detail URL for each record is the human
page
\verb{https://www.nve.no/konsesjon/konsesjonssaker/konsesjonssak?id=\{SoknadId\}&type=\{Type\}}.
}

\section{Attachments}{

Each detail page renders the case documents in one or more \code{div.n-filelist}
sections, each with an \verb{<h2>} section heading (e.g. \emph{Konsesjon}) and a list
of \verb{<a>} links to downloadable PDFs at
\verb{https://webfileservice.nve.no/API/PublishedFiles/Download/<saksnummer>/<fileId>}
(and a UUID variant \verb{.../Download/<uuid>/<saksnummer>/<fileId>}); no
authentication is needed. The handler scrapes those links (sidecar-first),
grouping the URLs by the section heading into per-section
\verb{attachment_urls_<slug>} columns (the DE / IT / SK pattern; the slug is the
ASCII-folded Norwegian heading) plus the deduplicated union in
\code{attachment_urls}. EIA documents are \strong{not} type-flagged in the markup, so
every case document is collected; the document label/title is kept verbatim
(in the link's \verb{<h3>}) so downstream can identify
"konsekvensutredning" / "KU" / "melding" by filename. A case with no
published files yields an empty \code{attachment_urls} vector, which is valid.
}

\section{Summary}{

The detail page renders a human-readable case summary in the main content
column (\code{div.n-col-7}) as a \code{div.n-mb-5} block. The handler extracts it into
the conventional \code{summary} column (whitespace-collapsed). The \code{n-mb-5}
utility class is reused by the sidebar and the file list, but those sit in the
sidebar column (\code{div.n-col-5}), so the selector is scoped to \code{div.n-col-7} to
isolate the summary. Cases that render no summary yield \code{NA} (valid).
}

\section{Filter coverage (v0.1)}{

\itemize{
\item \code{query} — forwarded \strong{server-side} as the API \code{filterText} parameter (the
getall API matches it against the case title/proponent and returns a
filtered list + \code{TotalCount}).
\item \code{date_range} — matched client-side against \code{date_published} (the case
\code{Dato}). \code{date_decision} is always \code{NA} (the API exposes no separate
